emscripten-core/emscripten @ 887305e67c41 (main, 2026-09-26): let cwrap's fast
path accept 'bigint' arguments.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of LEAP 71's PicoGK, binds the raw wasm exports directly and does not depend on
this change. It is offered upstream as a standalone fix.

The defect (src/lib/libccall.js:148). cwrap returns the raw export when every
argument type is 'number' or 'boolean' and the return type is not 'string'.
'bigint' is missing from that list, so one i64 parameter sends the whole binding
through ccall: a getCFunc lookup, the per-argument conversion loop and a stack
save/restore on every call. Under -sWASM_BIGINT, which is on by default, a
BigInt reaches the export unchanged, and ccall's toC table has no 'bigint'
entry, so the slow path does no useful work for these bindings.

Scope. Only the argument check changes. A boolean return taking the fast path
(cwrap returns the raw i32 where ccall returns Boolean(ret)) is already
reported as emscripten issue #27021 and fixed in PR #27023, so this patch
leaves the return check alone. The fast path also stays behind
#if !ASSERTIONS, which is a separate policy question.

Output values: unchanged. The same export is called with the same arguments;
only the wrapper is skipped.

Measured motivation (picovoxel, emsdk 5.0.1, Apple M2 Pro, 2026-07-26): 143 of
147 PicoGK exports take a 64-bit handle, so all of them were on the slow path.
Lattice_AddBeam cost 154.3 ns per call through cwrap and ccall against 56.4 ns
calling the export directly; Voxels_bIsEmpty 99.4 against 34.7 ns.

The test hunks extend test_ccall_cwrap_fast_path with an i64 function. They
have not been run against the emscripten test suite.

Target file sha256 prefix: src/lib/libccall.js f6197b37fbbd3f81.
Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` at the root of an emscripten checkout.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

--- a/src/lib/libccall.js
+++ b/src/lib/libccall.js
@@ -145,7 +145,7 @@
 #if !ASSERTIONS
     // When the function takes numbers and returns a number, we can just return
